\begin{equation}
\mathrm{EDD}_T = \max \bigl\{ d^*{+}1 : \forall\, d' \leq d^*,\; \mathrm{AUC}(d', d'{+}1) \geq T \bigr\}
\label{eq:edd}
\end{equation}
where $d^* \in \{0, 1, \dots, K{-}1\}$.\footnote{In our data, pairwise AUC decreases monotonically with depth across all configurations tested, so the universal quantifier ($\forall d' \leq d^*$) and the simpler $\max\{d^*{+}1 : \mathrm{AUC}(d^*, d^*{+}1) \geq T\}$ yield identical values.  The $\forall$ formulation is preferred because it guarantees a contiguous discriminable range, which the pointwise version does not when AUC is non-monotonic.}

tool_args.old_string:
\paragraph{EDD threshold sensitivity.}
The Effective Discriminable Depth depends on the AUC threshold $T$: at $T{=}0.80$, several cells lose one discriminable depth level relative to $T{=}0.75$.
The choice of $T$ is application-dependent and affects reported EDD values.

\paragraph{Additional caveats.}
Detection features degrade under domain shift \cite{pudasaini2026_detection_fails}; our depth features are likely susceptible.
\citet{bakshi2026_collapse_improvement} find that early recursion can temporarily \emph{improve} model quality, potentially violating the monotonicity assumption, and \citet{falahati2026_curated} show that curated synthetic data need not trigger collapse, attenuating the fingerprints we exploit.

